00:45:48.000 --> 00:46:33.000
Michael Rosenfeld says the data shows... Attitudes towards every kind of gay rights has increased in a similar way, not quite as fast, but in a similar way to marriage equality. And then what little data we have on trans rights also suggests that there's been a sharp increase in appreciation for the rights of trans people and I think one of the things that has been shown by the marriage equality movement is that if you're a gay or lesbian you have more rights than you used to have and that's those rights are important they have ramifications for everybody whether they want a marry or not for everybody whether they want to marry or not.
迈克尔·罗森费尔德说，数据显示……人们对各种同性恋权利的态度都在以类似的方式增长，虽然速度没那么快，但与婚姻平等相似。我们掌握的关于跨性别者权利的有限数据也表明，人们对跨性别者权利的欣赏急剧增加。我认为婚姻平等运动所展现的一点是，如果你是同性恋或女同性恋，你享有的权利比过去多了，这些权利很重要，它们对每个人都有影响，无论他们是否想结婚，对每个人都有影响，无论他们是否想结婚。

73
00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:56.000
Evan says he consults today with a number of other activist groups. He reminds them that each struggle for change is different, and that what works in one case won't always work in another. Having said that, there are commonalities and there certainly are elements of success that I now do try to share with others who come to me from different movements, different causes in different countries, including struggles over women's empowerment or racial justice
埃文说，他现在为许多其他活动人士团体提供咨询。他提醒他们，每一场争取变革的斗争都是不同的，一个案例中管用的方法在另一个案例中并不总是奏效。话虽如此，其中确实有共性，也确实有成功的要素，我现在正努力与那些来自不同运动、不同诉求、不同国家的向我求助的人分享这些要素，包括围绕妇女赋权或种族正义的斗争

74
00:46:56.000 --> 00:47:34.000
or the rights of immigrants, et cetera. And one of those is the importance of connecting with others, of finding a way to bring the other over to your side, to bring about empathy, understanding awareness. Not by sacrificing your identity, not by subordinating or disclaiming your difference, but by finding a way to bridge those differences and to evoke in people the shared values that will enable them to connect with your personal story, your personal reality, and the rightness of treating others the way you would want to be treated, the golden rule.
或是移民权利等。其中之一就是与他人建立联系的重要性，找到一种方法将对方争取到你这一边，激发同理心、理解和意识。不是通过牺牲你的身份，不是通过贬低或抹杀你的差异，而是通过找到一种方法来弥合这些差异，唤起人们共同的价值观，使他们能够与你的个人故事、你的个人现实产生共鸣，并认识到以你希望被对待的方式去对待他人的正确性，即黄金法则。

75
00:47:34.000 --> 00:48:10.000
And it's not easy to do this for anybody. It wasn't easy and it's not done for gay people or for marriage or for transgender people. And it's certainly not easy with regard to race. And yet our country teaches us that we have move, we have change, we have been able to make progress, not that we're done by any means, not that there isn't still ugliness and not that there are still opponents. But to ignore the progress is also to miss part of the story. Changemakers often have a choice.
对任何人来说，要做到这一点都不容易。这对同性恋者、婚姻或跨性别者来说过去不容易，现在也没有完成。在种族问题上当然更不容易。然而，我们的历史告诉我们，我们已经前行，我们已经改变，我们已经能够取得进展——这并不是说我们已经大功告成，并不是说不再有丑陋现象，也并不是说没有了反对者。但忽视这些进展同样也是遗漏了故事的一部分。变革者往往面临选择。

76
00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:42.000
Overthrow the old order, perhaps through violence or revolution, or reform the old order, reconcile and forgive. Which path you choose can be shaped by many factors, including how strong you are, and whether you need allies to succeed. Those who wield a sword and those who hold out the olive branch can both be successful. But if you look at the long sweep of history, the evidence suggests one approach has been more effective.
是通过暴力或革命推翻旧秩序，还是改革旧秩序，走向和解与宽恕。你选择哪条道路会受到许多因素的影响，包括你的实力如何，以及你是否需要盟友来取得成功。挥舞长剑的人和伸出橄榄枝的人都可以取得成功。但如果你纵观漫长的历史长河，证据表明其中一种方法更有效。

77
00:48:42.000 --> 00:49:22.000
The political scientists Maria Stefano and Erica Chenoweth have analyzed hundreds of protest movements around the world that occurred over the course of the 20th century. They found that violent movements succeeded about one quarter of the time. Non-violent movements succeeded twice as often. The campaign for marriage equality told its opponents, look, we share the same values. Instead of force, it disarmed. Instead of arguing, it co-opted. Instead of shaming, it's celebrated.
政治科学家玛丽亚·斯特凡和埃里卡·切诺韦思分析了20世纪发生的全球数百场抗议运动。他们发现非暴力运动的成功率大约是四分之一。非暴力运动的成功率则是前者的两倍。争取婚姻平等的运动告诉对手：看，我们拥有相同的价值观。它没有使用武力，而是解除了武装；没有进行争论，而是进行了吸纳；没有进行羞辱，而是进行了庆祝。

78
00:49:22.000 --> 00:50:24.000
The psychological brilliance of the strategy comes down to this. It's very difficult to fight with people who say they embrace your values. This week's show was produced by Path Shah. It was edited by Tara Boyle and Jenny Schmidt. Our team includes Raina Cohen, Laura Corral and Thomas Lut. Voice acting from Alexandria Masehdu, Galina Abdel Aziz, Maissa Alves de Silva, and Casey Myers. Special thanks to Keith Woods, Sammy Anigan, and Jean Demby. Our unsung hero is Lillian Federman. She's a scholar who wrote a comprehensive history
